.Dd September 27, 2026
.Dt SIGACTION 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm sigaction ,
.Nm sigprocmask ,
.Nm sigsuspend ,
.Nm sigpending ,
.Nm sigaltstack ,
.Nm rt_sigaction ,
.Nm rt_sigprocmask ,
.Nm rt_sigsuspend ,
.Nm rt_sigpending ,
.Nm sigreturn ,
.Nm rt_sigreturn ,
.Nm rt_sigtimedwait ,
.Nm pause
.Nd control signal dispositions and masks
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In signal.h
.Ft int
.Fn sigaction "int sig" "const struct sigaction *restrict act" "struct sigaction *restrict oldact"
.Ft int
.Fn sigprocmask "int how" "const sigset_t *restrict set" "sigset_t *restrict oldset"
.Ft int
.Fn sigsuspend "const sigset_t *mask"
.Ft int
.Fn sigpending "sigset_t *set"
.Ft int
.Fn sigaltstack "const stack_t *restrict ss" "stack_t *restrict old_ss"
.In unistd.h
.Ft int
.Fn pause void
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn sigaction
installs the disposition in
.Fa act
for signal
.Fa sig .
If
.Fa oldact
is non-null, it receives the previous disposition.
A null
.Fa act
leaves the disposition unchanged.
The kernel rejects even a query for
.Dv SIGKILL
or
.Dv SIGSTOP ;
neither can be caught, ignored, or blocked.
.Pp
The kernel accepts signal numbers 1 through 64.
Numbers 1 through 31 have their Linux i386 meanings.
Musl reserves numbers 32 through 34 and rejects them in
.Fn sigaction .
Pending signals occupy one bit each; repeated instances coalesce, and
the sender information of the first is what the handler receives.
There is no queue of real-time signals.
.Pp
The dispositions belong to the process and are shared by its threads.
The blocked mask, the alternate stack and a set of pending signals belong
to each thread; a signal sent to the process is pending for the process,
and any thread that does not block it may take it.
The thread woken to take it is the thread the sender named, or else the
first thread, that does not block it and is not stopped by job control or
by a tracer.
When a thread's mask comes to block such a signal while it is pending,
through
.Fn sigprocmask ,
a handler's mask or its return, or the mask a wait installs or puts back,
another thread that can take it is woken to.
A thread takes its own pending signals before the process's.
.Ss Dispositions
.Va sa_handler
selects
.Dv SIG_DFL ,
.Dv SIG_IGN ,
or a handler address.
Installing
.Dv SIG_IGN
also discards an instance already pending, the process's and each
thread's.
The default actions are:
.Bl -tag -width "Terminate"
.It Ignore
.Dv SIGCHLD , SIGURG , SIGWINCH ,
and
.Dv SIGCONT .
.It Stop
.Dv SIGSTOP , SIGTSTP , SIGTTIN ,
and
.Dv SIGTTOU .
.It Core
.Dv SIGQUIT , SIGILL , SIGTRAP , SIGABRT , SIGBUS , SIGFPE , SIGSEGV ,
.Dv SIGXCPU , SIGXFSZ ,
and
.Dv SIGSYS ,
which terminate the process and leave a core file where
.Xr core 5
allows one.
.It Terminate
Every other accepted signal number.
.El
.Pp
A stop stops the whole process: the thread that takes the stop signal
records the stop, and every thread parks as it next returns to user mode.
Posting
.Dv SIGCONT
resumes a stopped process, every thread of it, and removes pending stop
signals, regardless of its disposition or blocked status.
A stop signal removes pending
.Dv SIGCONT .
.Dv SIGKILL
makes a stopped process runnable so it can terminate.
A terminating or core default ends every thread of the process, as
.Xr exit 2
describes for
.Nm exit_group .
Stop and continue events notify the parent with
.Dv SIGCHLD
and are reported by
.Xr wait 2 .
The booted init process cannot be stopped.
.Pp
Signals act on return to user mode from a system call or interrupt.
The lowest-numbered of the thread's own pending, unblocked signals is
selected first, and when it has none, the lowest-numbered of the
process's.
An ignored signal does not interrupt a blocking wait.
A caught, stopping, or terminating signal that is not blocked can
interrupt a wait;
an operation that has transferred data may return a partial count.
A system call interrupted before it transferred anything is restarted
when no handler runs, as after a stop and a continue, and when the
handler that runs has
.Dv SA_RESTART ;
otherwise it fails with
.Er EINTR .
The calls that wait for a signal or a deadline are never restarted:
.Fn pause ,
.Fn sigsuspend ,
.Fn nanosleep ,
.Fn clock_nanosleep ,
.Fn poll ,
.Fn ppoll ,
.Fn select
and
.Fn pselect .
.Pp
A user-mode CPU exception raises its signal as Linux raises it:
.Dv SIGSEGV
with
.Dv SEGV_MAPERR
or
.Dv SEGV_ACCERR
and the faulting address for a page fault,
.Dv SIGILL
with
.Dv ILL_ILLOPN ,
.Dv SIGFPE
with the code of the error the unit raised,
.Dv SIGBUS
for a segment or alignment fault,
.Dv SIGTRAP
for the
.Li int3
instruction, which user mode may execute, and for a single step, and
.Dv SIGSEGV
with
.Dv SI_KERNEL
for a protection fault.
A signal the process blocks or ignores cannot recover it: the disposition
returns to default, the signal is unblocked, and the process dies.
A handler runs otherwise, and the faulting instruction runs again when the
handler returns.
.Ss Handlers
While a handler runs, its
.Va sa_mask
is added to the blocked mask.
The delivered signal is also blocked unless
.Dv SA_NODEFER
is set.
.Dv SIGKILL
and
.Dv SIGSTOP
are removed from this mask.
.Dv SA_RESETHAND
resets the disposition to default before entering the handler.
.Pp
With
.Dv SA_SIGINFO ,
the handler receives pointers to a
.Vt siginfo_t
and a
.Vt ucontext_t
in Linux's i386 layouts.
The
.Vt siginfo_t
has the signal number and code:
.Dv SI_USER
with the sender's process and real user IDs for
.Fn kill ,
.Dv SI_TKILL
with them for
.Fn tkill
and
.Fn tgkill ,
the codes above with the address for a fault,
.Dv CLD_EXITED ,
.Dv CLD_KILLED ,
.Dv CLD_STOPPED
or
.Dv CLD_CONTINUED
with the child's process ID, real user ID and status for
.Dv SIGCHLD ,
and
.Dv SI_KERNEL
for what the kernel sends, such as terminal signals and
.Dv SIGALRM .
The
.Vt ucontext_t
holds the interrupted registers, the address of the floating-point state
in Linux's
.Vt struct _fpstate_32
form, the mask from before the handler, and the alternate stack.
A handler that changes the saved registers or floating-point state
returns to what it wrote.
.Pp
All action flags are stored and returned.
.Dv SA_RESTART
restarts an interrupted system call as described above, and
.Dv SA_ONSTACK
runs the handler on the alternate stack when one is set and not in use.
A frame that would run off the bottom of the alternate stack is not
built, and the process ends with
.Dv SIGSEGV ,
as on Linux.
Without
.Dv SA_RESTORER ,
the handler returns to code in its own frame that makes the return call.
.Dv SA_NOCLDSTOP
and
.Dv SA_NOCLDWAIT
have no special effect.
.Ss Masks and waiting
.Fn sigprocmask
returns the previous mask through a non-null
.Fa oldset .
If
.Fa set
is non-null,
.Fa how
selects
.Dv SIG_BLOCK
(0) to add its signals,
.Dv SIG_UNBLOCK
(1) to remove them, or
.Dv SIG_SETMASK
(2) to replace the mask.
With a null
.Fa set ,
the mask is unchanged and
.Fa how
is ignored.
.Pp
.Fn pause
waits for a signal that can act.
.Fn sigsuspend
installs
.Fa mask
for the wait.
The handler of the signal that ends it runs under
.Fa mask ,
modified by the handler's own masking rules, and the previous mask comes
back when the handler returns; when no handler runs it comes back on the
return from the call.
.Pp
.Fn sigpending
reports the signals pending for the calling thread or its process while
the thread blocks them.
.Pp
.Nm rt_sigtimedwait ,
number 177, which
.Fn sigwait ,
.Fn sigwaitinfo
and
.Fn sigtimedwait
make, waits for a signal of a set to be pending for the thread or its
process and takes it off its pending set without running its handler:
the lowest of the thread's own, or, when it has none of the set pending,
the lowest of the process's.
Its siginfo is stored unless the pointer is null, and its number is
returned.
While the call waits the signals of the set count as unblocked, so a
signal sent to the process wakes this thread.
A timeout is rounded up to whole 10 ms ticks and limited to 2147483646
ticks, about 248.55 days, as for
.Xr nanosleep 2 ;
it ends the wait with
.Er EAGAIN ,
one of zero polls, and another signal the thread acts on ends it with
.Er EINTR ;
the call is not restarted.
.Dv SIGKILL
and
.Dv SIGSTOP
cannot be waited for.
.Ss Alternate stack
.Fn sigaltstack
sets the stack a handler with
.Dv SA_ONSTACK
runs on, and reports the previous one through a non-null
.Fa old_ss :
its base, its size, and
.Dv SS_DISABLE
when none is set or
.Dv SS_ONSTACK
while the process runs on it.
.Dv SS_DISABLE
in
.Fa ss
removes the stack.
A process on its alternate stack cannot change it, and a stack smaller
than
.Dv MINSIGSTKSZ ,
2048 bytes, is refused.
.Dv SS_AUTODISARM
is accepted and has no effect.
A child made by
.Xr fork 2
inherits the stack, and
.Xr execve 2
removes it.
.Ss System call interface
Musl's
.Vt sigset_t
occupies 128 bytes.
The kernel uses an 8-byte set: two 32-bit words for signals 1 through 32
and 33 through 64, with bit
.Li sig-1
representing a signal.
Musl passes this smaller size to the kernel.
.Pp
The i386 system call numbers and register arguments are:
.Bl -tag -width "rt_sigprocmask (175)"
.It Nm rt_sigaction No (174)
EBX is the signal, ECX the new action pointer, EDX the old action pointer,
and ESI the set size, which must be 8.
The kernel action is five 32-bit words: handler address, flags, restorer
address, and the two mask words.
.Fn sigaction
converts between this layout and the public structure.
.It Nm rt_sigprocmask No (175)
EBX is
.Fa how ,
ECX the new set pointer, EDX the old set pointer, and ESI the set size, 8.
Musl's
.Fn sigprocmask
uses this call.
.It Nm rt_sigsuspend No (179)
EBX points to the temporary set and ECX is its size, 8.
Musl's
.Fn sigsuspend
uses this call.
.It Nm rt_sigpending No (176)
EBX points to the set and ECX is its size, at most 8.
.It Nm sigprocmask No (126)
The older form of
.Nm rt_sigprocmask ,
with signals 1 through 32 in one word: EBX is
.Fa how ,
ECX the new set pointer and EDX the old set pointer.
GNU libc's
.Fn getcontext
uses it.
.It Nm sigaltstack No (186)
EBX points to the new
.Vt stack_t
and ECX to the old one; either may be null.
.It Nm pause No (29)
No arguments.
.It Nm sigreturn No (119)
Musl's plain-handler restorer pops the signal argument before issuing
the call.
The saved frame begins 8 bytes below the user stack pointer.
.It Nm rt_sigreturn No (173)
Musl's
.Dv SA_SIGINFO
restorer issues the call without popping the signal argument.
The saved frame begins 4 bytes below the user stack pointer.
.El
.Pp
Musl and GNU libc declare no public functions for the
.Li rt_*
calls or
.Nm sigreturn ;
each supplies a restorer address when installing an action.
.Pp
The frames are Linux's: a plain handler's holds the return address, the
signal number, a
.Vt struct sigcontext
and the upper word of the mask to restore; an
.Dv SA_SIGINFO
handler's holds the return address, the signal number, the two pointers,
the
.Vt siginfo_t
and the
.Vt ucontext_t .
The floating-point state lies above the frame, 64-byte aligned.
The handler starts with the signal number in EAX and, with
.Dv SA_SIGINFO ,
the two pointers in EDX and ECX, with the direction and trap flags clear
and the initial floating-point state.
Both return calls restore the saved general registers, instruction and
stack pointers, the processor flags a program may change, the
floating-point state, and the saved mask; the rt form restores the
alternate stack as well.
Segment selectors are restricted to the ones this kernel has.
An unreadable frame, or a floating-point state with a reserved MXCSR bit
set, terminates the process with
.Dv SIGSEGV .
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn sigaction
and
.Fn sigprocmask
return 0 on success.
On failure they return -1 and set
.Va errno .
.Fn pause
and
.Fn sigsuspend
return -1 with
.Va errno
set to
.Er EINTR
when the wait ends and execution continues.
The kernel calls return negative error numbers.
The signal-return calls resume saved execution, including its EAX value.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/sigprobe.c
installs a
.Dv SIGUSR1
handler, blocks and posts the signal, then unblocks it and checks that
the handler runs and returns through
.Nm rt_sigreturn .
.Pa tests/musl/sigframemusl.c
recovers from a write to a read-only page in its handler, restarts a read
with
.Dv SA_RESTART ,
runs a handler on the alternate stack, catches
.Li int3 ,
and checks the pending set, the masks of
.Fn sigsuspend
and the old
.Nm sigprocmask ,
and
.Dv SIGCHLD Ns 's
information.
.Pa tests/musl/callsmusl.c
takes a blocked signal with
.Fn sigtimedwait
and times out with nothing pending.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[EINVAL]"
.It Bq Er EFAULT
An action or signal-set pointer cannot be read or written as required.
.It Bq Er EINTR
The wait in
.Fn pause
or
.Fn sigsuspend
ended because a signal could act.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
The signal is outside 1 through 64, is
.Dv SIGKILL
or
.Dv SIGSTOP ,
or is reserved by musl;
a kernel set size is not 8, or more than 8 for
.Nm rt_sigpending ;
.Fa how
is invalid when a new mask is supplied; or
.Fn sigaltstack
was given flags other than those above.
.It Bq Er ENOMEM
The alternate stack is smaller than
.Dv MINSIGSTKSZ .
.It Bq Er EPERM
The process tried to change its alternate stack while running on it.
.It Bq Er ESRCH
There is no current process.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr kill 2 ,
.Xr wait 2 ,
.Xr core 5
